WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.290 --> 00:00:02.750
- A report came out that
a conservative majority

00:00:02.750 --> 00:00:04.460
on the United States Supreme Court

00:00:04.460 --> 00:00:07.460
is ready to overturn Roe V Wade

00:00:07.460 --> 00:00:10.840
and uproot decades of precedent affirming,

00:00:10.840 --> 00:00:12.270
decades of precedent,

00:00:12.270 --> 00:00:14.640
affirming a woman's right to an abortion.

00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:15.490
- [Narrator] That's Chuck Schumer

00:00:15.490 --> 00:00:18.280
speaking about a leaked
draft of a court opinion,

00:00:18.280 --> 00:00:21.620
originally obtained and
reported by Politico.

00:00:21.620 --> 00:00:24.980
The draft, which was circulated
among Justices in February,

00:00:24.980 --> 00:00:27.700
suggests that the court
is poised to overturn

00:00:27.700 --> 00:00:30.790
the decades old decision
on the right to abortion.

00:00:30.790 --> 00:00:32.960
Shortly after the leak, the Supreme Court

00:00:32.960 --> 00:00:36.050
released a statement confirming
the document's authenticity,

00:00:36.050 --> 00:00:39.610
but added that it did not
represent a decision by the court.

00:00:39.610 --> 00:00:43.150
- What we're seeing is a
snapshot and it could change,

00:00:43.150 --> 00:00:45.560
even though it's written
in this very polished way

00:00:45.560 --> 00:00:47.310
that makes it look like a final opinion.

00:00:47.310 --> 00:00:49.710
- Writing an important legal opinion,

00:00:49.710 --> 00:00:54.530
which is going to have an
effect on the whole country,

00:00:54.530 --> 00:00:55.960
takes a bit of time.

00:00:55.960 --> 00:00:57.300
- [Narrator] The leaked draft represents

00:00:57.300 --> 00:00:58.650
one step in a process

00:00:58.650 --> 00:01:01.020
that can involve months of deliberation.

00:01:01.020 --> 00:01:02.800
Here's a look at how the Supreme Court

00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:04.500
makes decisions on cases

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:07.323
and how their rulings affect
lawmaking in Congress.

00:01:08.230 --> 00:01:10.230
Once the Supreme Court accepts a case,

00:01:10.230 --> 00:01:11.690
Justices review briefs

00:01:11.690 --> 00:01:14.450
and hear oral arguments from both sides.

00:01:14.450 --> 00:01:16.960
In December, the court
heard oral arguments for

00:01:16.960 --> 00:01:19.740
Dobbs V Jackson Women's
Health Organization,

00:01:19.740 --> 00:01:22.430
a case that considers a
state law in Mississippi

00:01:22.430 --> 00:01:25.390
seeking to ban abortion after 15 weeks.

00:01:25.390 --> 00:01:27.660
As part of the case,
the court is considering

00:01:27.660 --> 00:01:30.610
whether Roe V Wade should be overruled.

00:01:30.610 --> 00:01:32.400
Within days of an oral argument,

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:34.350
the Justices hold a private meeting,

00:01:34.350 --> 00:01:35.670
known as the conference,

00:01:35.670 --> 00:01:37.300
where they explain their views on a case

00:01:37.300 --> 00:01:40.790
and take a straw vote, or a
preliminary vote, on the ruling.

00:01:40.790 --> 00:01:42.550
The Senior Justice and the majority

00:01:42.550 --> 00:01:44.660
decides who writes the court's opinion,

00:01:44.660 --> 00:01:47.040
which is a written explanation
of what they decided

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:48.590
and the reasoning behind it.

00:01:48.590 --> 00:01:50.910
In this case, Justice Samuel Alito

00:01:50.910 --> 00:01:52.630
was assigned to write the draft.

00:01:52.630 --> 00:01:54.410
- A majority opinion of the Supreme Court

00:01:54.410 --> 00:01:57.580
decides what the law is
in the United States.

00:01:57.580 --> 00:01:59.890
It's the last word when it comes to

00:01:59.890 --> 00:02:02.410
interpreting the United
States Constitution.

00:02:02.410 --> 00:02:04.270
- [Narrator] The majority
opinion is the only one

00:02:04.270 --> 00:02:05.770
that holds legal weight,

00:02:05.770 --> 00:02:08.010
but Justices can write individual opinions

00:02:08.010 --> 00:02:09.620
explaining their stance.

00:02:09.620 --> 00:02:11.730
Those who agree, but
have additional ideas,

00:02:11.730 --> 00:02:13.560
can write a concurring opinion.

00:02:13.560 --> 00:02:16.700
And those who disagree can
draft a dissenting opinion.

00:02:16.700 --> 00:02:18.730
Those opinions circulate through the court

00:02:18.730 --> 00:02:21.810
and Justices decide whether
or not to sign onto them.

00:02:21.810 --> 00:02:24.550
This process can last
several weeks or months.

00:02:24.550 --> 00:02:26.590
During this time, they give feedback on

00:02:26.590 --> 00:02:29.900
the wording of the opinion and
the document can be edited.

00:02:29.900 --> 00:02:31.450
- From people in the majority,

00:02:31.450 --> 00:02:32.920
they're gonna be saying what they like,

00:02:32.920 --> 00:02:35.380
what they don't like, what
they wanna see emphasized,

00:02:35.380 --> 00:02:37.950
what they think is going too far,

00:02:37.950 --> 00:02:39.350
that kind of thing.

00:02:39.350 --> 00:02:41.960
And they'll send notes or corrections.

00:02:41.960 --> 00:02:43.770
They'll make suggestions.

00:02:43.770 --> 00:02:46.193
Sometimes during that process,

00:02:47.570 --> 00:02:50.110
a Justice can lose a majority.

00:02:50.110 --> 00:02:51.480
- [Narrator] While it
doesn't happen often,

00:02:51.480 --> 00:02:53.000
Justices have changed their minds

00:02:53.000 --> 00:02:54.950
while the opinion was being drafted.

00:02:54.950 --> 00:02:57.380
In 2012, Chief Justice John Roberts

00:02:57.380 --> 00:03:00.240
initially voted to strike
down the Affordable Care Act,

00:03:00.240 --> 00:03:02.960
but ultimately came to see
the law as constitutional.

00:03:02.960 --> 00:03:04.530
That's what changed the outcome

00:03:04.530 --> 00:03:07.210
to a 5/4 vote upholding the statute.

00:03:07.210 --> 00:03:11.460
- Things can change up until
the last minute, you know,

00:03:11.460 --> 00:03:14.820
until the court officially,
formally, publicly

00:03:14.820 --> 00:03:18.790
releases an opinion, it
has not made a decision.

00:03:18.790 --> 00:03:20.860
- [Narrator] At least
five out of nine Justices

00:03:20.860 --> 00:03:23.370
need to sign on to approve the opinion.

00:03:23.370 --> 00:03:26.480
The draft is kept completely
private until this point.

00:03:26.480 --> 00:03:27.930
Then it is released to the public

00:03:27.930 --> 00:03:29.950
and treated as constitutional law,

00:03:29.950 --> 00:03:32.850
which federal and state
legislatures are bound to follow.

00:03:32.850 --> 00:03:36.240
- Sometimes when the Supreme
Court makes a decision,

00:03:36.240 --> 00:03:39.850
there is a reaction and
Congress takes some steps

00:03:39.850 --> 00:03:42.330
to respond to what the court has done.

00:03:42.330 --> 00:03:43.360
- [Narrator] If the draft opinion

00:03:43.360 --> 00:03:46.420
in Dobbs V Jackson Women's
Health Organization holds,

00:03:46.420 --> 00:03:48.500
it will be the rare
instance where the court

00:03:48.500 --> 00:03:51.430
rescinds a right it
previously has recognized.

00:03:51.430 --> 00:03:54.020
- What this decision is
purporting to do is to

00:03:54.020 --> 00:03:56.640
take away a prior Supreme Court decision

00:03:56.640 --> 00:03:58.810
recognizing a constitutional right

00:03:58.810 --> 00:04:00.820
and that constitutional right restricted

00:04:00.820 --> 00:04:02.230
what legislatures could do,

00:04:02.230 --> 00:04:04.560
be it state legislatures, or Congress.

00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:05.840
- [Narrator] If the majority of Justices

00:04:05.840 --> 00:04:08.430
sign onto the opinion,
abortion restrictions that have

00:04:08.430 --> 00:04:10.810
previously been unenforceable under Roe,

00:04:10.810 --> 00:04:13.290
like those in Florida,
Texas, and Michigan,

00:04:13.290 --> 00:04:15.060
could start being enforced.

00:04:15.060 --> 00:04:16.410
Some states could act to

00:04:16.410 --> 00:04:18.510
preserve the right to reproductive choice.

00:04:18.510 --> 00:04:21.170
Lawmakers in New York and
California said that they would

00:04:21.170 --> 00:04:23.330
push to amend their state constitutions

00:04:23.330 --> 00:04:26.640
to cement existing laws that
protect abortion rights.

00:04:26.640 --> 00:04:28.430
Senate Majority Leader, Chuck Schumer,

00:04:28.430 --> 00:04:31.670
has stated Democrats'
intention to codify Roe V Wade

00:04:31.670 --> 00:04:33.010
at the federal level,

00:04:33.010 --> 00:04:36.470
but any bill faces major
hurdles in a divided Congress.

00:04:36.470 --> 00:04:38.840
- It is my intention for the Senate

00:04:38.840 --> 00:04:41.120
to hold a vote on legislation

00:04:41.120 --> 00:04:44.670
to codify the right to an abortion in law.

00:04:44.670 --> 00:04:48.353
- If the Supreme Court were
to overrule Roe V Wade,

00:04:49.570 --> 00:04:51.930
that's a kind of unusual situation.

00:04:51.930 --> 00:04:54.390
'Cause you know, it's very
hard to think of a time

00:04:54.390 --> 00:04:57.430
that the court recognized
an individual right

00:04:57.430 --> 00:04:59.690
and then took it away.

00:04:59.690 --> 00:05:02.370
And then people who
still wanted that right

00:05:02.370 --> 00:05:04.730
had to enact it legislatively.

00:05:04.730 --> 00:05:06.420
- [Narrator] The court
has until the end of June

00:05:06.420 --> 00:05:07.680
to make a final decision

00:05:07.680 --> 00:05:10.052
and experts expect that abortion will be

00:05:10.052 --> 00:05:12.852
one of the final rulings
before the court takes a break.

